      <span class="kicker">Case Study</span>
      <h1 class="essay-title">When the Framework Outruns the Funding</h1>
      <p class="essay-sub">
        Implementing Results-Based Accountability across a 15-partner network
        at a Promise Neighborhood backbone organization
      </p>
      <p class="essay-byline">
        Madeleine Smith · Reflections on Evaluation Practice
      </p>
    </header>

    <hr class="rule" />

    <section class="essay-section" id="context">
      <h2 class="section-label">Context</h2>
      <p>
        I spent two years as the Evaluation Analyst inside a Promise
        Neighborhood network. Promise Neighborhoods are federally funded,
        place-based initiatives where a backbone organization serves as the
        fiscal agent for a network of community-based partners. Ours had
        fifteen.
      </p>
      <p>
        The measurement architecture had over 100 KPIs at the network level.
        Each partner organization reported on a subset of 10 to 25 metrics
        depending on their programming. I built and ran the infrastructure that
        synthesized data across the network.
      </p>

      <dl class="stats">
        <div class="stat stat--primary">
          <dt class="stat-num">100+</dt>
          <dd class="stat-caption">Network-level KPIs</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <dt class="stat-num">15</dt>
          <dd class="stat-caption">Partner organizations in the network</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <dt class="stat-num">10–25</dt>
          <dd class="stat-caption">Metrics per partner org</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <hr class="rule" />

    <section class="essay-section" id="methodology">
      <h2 class="section-label">Methodology</h2>
      <p>
        The methodological frame was Results-Based Accountability, which is
        common in Promise Neighborhood work. The network-wide measurement
        covered four strategic areas: health, education, economic mobility, and
        housing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mixed-methods piece worth explaining is the Turn the Curve
        reflection cycle. These happened annually inside the backbone
        organization. Staff across departments reviewed quantitative trends and
        added qualitative interpretation. Program staff described what they saw
        on the ground. Development staff connected findings to funder
        priorities. Communications staff thought about how to talk about the
        data publicly. That qualitative layer is where most of the actual
        learning happened. These sessions did not include partner
        organizations. We'll come back to this.
      </p>
      <p>
        We used a participatory data review process to bring different
        perspectives into the same conversation, especially across staff with
        very different relationships to data. Governance documents and SOPs
        were co-built across departments so the framework was legible to
        everyone using it.
      </p>

