    <section class="essay-section" id="what-worked">
      <h2 class="section-label">What Worked Internally</h2>
      <p>A few things worked inside the backbone's data culture.</p>
      <p>
        The cross-departmental review sessions brought program staff,
        evaluation, development, and communications into the same conversation
        about what the numbers meant. That coordination didn't exist before.
        When a metric moved, program staff had first read on why. Their
        interpretation wasn't an afterthought added to a report. It was part of
        how the organization understood what was happening.
      </p>
      <p>
        The participatory data review kept those conversations structured
        across staff who related to data very differently. And the governance
        documents, co-built across departments, meant the framework was legible
        to everyone using it inside the organization.
      </p>
      <p>
        That cross-departmental review produced the most coordinated read of
        its own programs the organization had ever generated.
      </p>

